WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.949
Once a month, an inspector from the town hall visits the corner shop, and goes through the Saturday breakfast box.

2
00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:12.228
Her check depends on what she finds.

3
00:00:12.228 --> 00:00:13.558
The date on the milk.

4
00:00:13.558 --> 00:00:15.267
The price label on the jam.

5
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:19.001
Inside is a smaller box, so she opens it, and starts again.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.298
It's the breakfast box from the Composite lesson, with three kinds of goods.

7
00:00:24.298 --> 00:00:27.080
An item: a packet with a price, like the jam.

8
00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.666
Fresh food, which also knows its days left, like the milk.

9
00:00:30.666 --> 00:00:32.768
And a box, which holds more goods.

10
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:35.574
The kinds hardly ever change.

11
00:00:35.574 --> 00:00:37.218
But the jobs keep coming.

12
00:00:37.218 --> 00:00:41.033
The price, where fresh food is half price on its last day.

13
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:42.480
The inspector's check.

14
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.664
The label printer.

15
00:00:43.664 --> 00:00:45.440
The weight for the courier.

16
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.594
Picture a grid.

17
00:00:46.594 --> 00:00:51.565
One row per kind, one column per job, and every cell a small piece of code.

18
00:00:51.565 --> 00:00:53.620
So where should the cells live?

19
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.014
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

20
00:00:57.014 --> 00:01:00.896
Why not give each class a price method, and an inspect method?

21
00:01:00.896 --> 00:01:02.900
Isn't that what objects are for?

22
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.772
It works, and most of us start there.

23
00:01:05.772 --> 00:01:07.579
Each class holds its own row.

24
00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:10.569
And a new kind of goods is cheap: one new class.

25
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.326
But new jobs arrive every month.

26
00:01:13.326 --> 00:01:18.532
A new column means opening Item, Fresh and Box, and adding a method to each.

27
00:01:18.532 --> 00:01:20.519
Next spring, all three again.

28
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:26.369
The goods get a new method every spring, like a fresh coat of paint, whether they need one or not.

29
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:28.878
What we'd like is the other way round.

30
00:01:28.878 --> 00:01:31.901
A new job in one place, and the goods left alone.

31
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.365
And that's exactly what the inspector does.

32
00:01:35.365 --> 00:01:36.265
Let's map her.

33
00:01:36.265 --> 00:01:38.002
The inspector is a visitor.

34
00:01:38.002 --> 00:01:41.667
This spring's checklist is one visitor class: one column.

35
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:45.204
A different check for each kind is one method per kind.

36
00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:49.963
And each thing opens its door to her with one small method, called accept.

37
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:52.195
Here it is in Java.

38
00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:54.783
GoodsVisitor has one method per kind.

39
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:56.951
Price is one job, in one class.

40
00:01:56.951 --> 00:01:58.630
An item costs its cents.

41
00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.917
Fresh food is half price when no days are left.

42
00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:04.155
And a box adds up what it holds.

43
00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.023
Kotlin has the same shape.

44
00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:10.896
Each kind of goods gets one small accept, which calls its own visit method.

45
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.511
Now follow one call: breakfast.accept(new Price()).

46
00:02:15.511 --> 00:02:19.731
Hop one: the box's own accept runs, and it calls visitBox.

47
00:02:19.731 --> 00:02:24.679
Hop two: visitBox asks each thing inside to accept the same visitor.

48
00:02:24.679 --> 00:02:26.935
The rye bread calls visitFresh.

49
00:02:26.935 --> 00:02:28.681
The jam calls visitItem.

50
00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.083
And the smaller box starts again.

51
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.585
Choosing the code by two things at once, the kind and the job, is called double dispatch.

52
00:02:37.585 --> 00:02:39.064
Accept picks the kind.

53
00:02:39.064 --> 00:02:40.409
Visit picks the job.

54
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.253
So what does the whole box cost?

55
00:02:44.253 --> 00:02:47.762
Rye bread, 250 cents, two days left.

56
00:02:47.762 --> 00:02:48.639
Jam, 300.

57
00:02:48.639 --> 00:02:49.517
Tea, 450.

58
00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:52.344
Milk, 120, with no days left.

59
00:02:52.344 --> 00:02:54.293
Pause and add it up.

60
00:02:57.733 --> 00:03:01.286
The milk is on its last day, so it counts for 60.

61
00:03:01.286 --> 00:03:06.216
And the lesson's VisitorVsSwitch printed: breakfast box, 1060 cents.

62
00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:09.828
Now the shop starts selling frozen peas.

63
00:03:09.828 --> 00:03:11.713
A new kind of goods: Frozen.

64
00:03:11.713 --> 00:03:13.532
What does the visitor need?

65
00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:18.353
Here the inspector picture breaks.

66
00:03:18.353 --> 00:03:20.376
A real inspector can improvise.

67
00:03:20.376 --> 00:03:24.489
A visitor can't: it has no method for a kind it never heard of.

68
00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:31.669
So the interface needs visitFrozen, Frozen needs an accept, and every visitor ever written needs a new method.

69
00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:35.095
That's a lot of paperwork for a bag of frozen peas.

70
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.955
This trade has a name: the expression problem.

71
00:03:38.955 --> 00:03:44.030
With methods in each class, new kinds are cheap and new jobs touch every class.

72
00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.664
With a visitor, it's the other way round.

73
00:03:46.664 --> 00:03:48.784
Pick the side you expect to grow.

74
00:03:48.784 --> 00:03:50.519
In the shop, the jobs grow.

75
00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:52.189
That's the visitor's side.

76
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.794
Today there's a lighter way.

77
00:03:54.794 --> 00:04:00.780
A sealed interface lists every type allowed to implement it, so the compiler knows the whole set.

78
00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:06.889
And pattern matching lets a switch, or a when, test which type a value is, and take its fields out.

79
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.637
So each job becomes one function, with no accept and no visitor interface.

80
00:04:12.637 --> 00:04:17.605
The guard, when days is zero, puts the last day rule right where it applies.

81
00:04:18.533 --> 00:04:21.288
And the inspector is one more function.

82
00:04:21.288 --> 00:04:27.291
The demo prices the box both ways: 1060 cents with the visitor, 1060 with the switch.

83
00:04:27.291 --> 00:04:30.116
And the inspector found: milk, last day.

84
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.851
Now add Frozen to the sealed set, and build.

85
00:04:33.851 --> 00:04:37.285
Kotlin answers: 'when' expression must be exhaustive.

86
00:04:37.285 --> 00:04:40.330
Add the 'is Frozen' branch or an 'else' branch.

87
00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:45.384
And Java says: the switch expression does not cover all possible input values.

88
00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:49.244
Visitor style: the interface, an accept, and every visitor.

89
00:04:49.244 --> 00:04:53.205
Switch style: one branch per function, each one listed for you.

90
00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:56.432
So when is a visitor still right?

91
00:04:56.432 --> 00:05:07.437
When the kinds are open to code you don't compile, or when a library hands you one, like Java's annotation processing, or ASM, a library for compiled classes.

92
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.151
Even the design notes for Java's pattern matching say so, in these words.

93
00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:13.521
So here's the rule.

94
00:05:13.521 --> 00:05:18.148
For a closed set of kinds you own, a sealed switch does the visitor's job.

95
00:05:18.148 --> 00:05:20.524
Keep Visitor for sets you can't close.

96
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.502
The inspector still comes every month.

97
00:05:23.502 --> 00:05:27.152
She just doesn't need the goods to hold the door for her any more.

98
00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:31.610
So, isn't a method on each class what objects are for?

99
00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.250
When the kinds grow, yes.

100
00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:38.433
When the jobs grow, give each job one place: one function with a sealed switch.

101
00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:39.359
Quick check.

102
00:05:39.359 --> 00:05:44.358
The shop adds a new job every month, and a new kind of goods about once a year.

103
00:05:44.358 --> 00:05:46.193
Which should be cheap to add?

104
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.021
New jobs.

105
00:05:50.021 --> 00:05:53.057
So one function per job, over a sealed type.

106
00:05:53.057 --> 00:05:56.161
The compiler lists the rare new kind for you.
